{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1127 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0d3b6111df99a038774bf1ce5c3e99ffc3ee5990e7af7f1dcf1d01340d037ea2" :edb/db-before-t 1126 :edb/db-after-t 1127 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534439 50 #inst "2026-10-07T04:00:57.123Z" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096390 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-\\sin^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534439 false] [17592186096390 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-\\sin^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096390 1145 "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad  \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096390 1145 "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\cos^2 x = 1-\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{1}{1 - \\sin^{2} x}dx &= ∫\\frac{1}{\\cos^{2} x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫(\\frac{1}{\\cos x})^{2}dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\sec^{2} xdx \\\\\n&= \\tan x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 false] [17592186096391 1100 "Calculate $\\, \\displaystyle{\\int} \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534439 false] [17592186096391 1100 "Calculate $\\, \\displaystyle{\\int} \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096391 1145 "First, we select the identity that contains a $\\tan^2{x}$ term. So we can use the identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2x =\\sec^2x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x =\\sec^2 x -1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\tan x -x+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 false] [17592186096391 1145 "First, we select the identity that contains a $\\tan^2{x}$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2x =\\sec^2x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x =\\sec^2 x -1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t&=\\tan x -x+C.\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096392 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(5\\cot^2 x -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534439 false] [17592186096392 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(5\\cot^2 x -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096392 1145 "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+\\cot^2x =\\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nRearranging this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\cot^2 x &=\\csc^2 x -1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\left(5\\cot^2 x -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\bigg(5\\left(\\csc^2 x -1 \\right) -x^2\\bigg) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(5\\csc^2 x -5 -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &=-5\\cot x -5x-\\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 false] [17592186096392 1145 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+\\cot^2x =\\csc^2 x.$ Rearranging this gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\cot^2 x &=\\csc^2 x -1.\n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\left(5\\cot^2 x -x^2\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\bigg(5\\left(\\csc^2 x -1 \\right) -x^2\\bigg) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t&=\\int \\left(5\\csc^2 x -5 -x^2\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t&=-5\\cot x -5x-\\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096393 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d} x$." 13194139534439 false] [17592186096393 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d} x.$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096393 1145 "From the identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 \\theta =\\sec^2 \\theta\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\theta = 3x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 1+\\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x)\\ \\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x) -1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x&= 2\\int \\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &= 2\\int \\left(\\sec^2 (3x) -1\\right) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &= 2\\left(\\int \\sec^2 (3x)\\, \\textrm{d}x -\\int1 \\, \\textrm{d}x\\right) \\\\ &= 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\tan (3x) -x\\right)+C \\\\ &= \\dfrac{2}{3}\\tan (3x) -2x+C. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 false] [17592186096393 1145 "From the identity  $1+\\tan^2 \\theta =\\sec^2 \\theta$ with $\\theta = 3x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t1+\\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x)\\\n\t\t\\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x) -1.\n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x&= 2\\int \\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n                                                                &= 2\\int \\left(\\sec^2 (3x) -1\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n                                                                 &= 2\\left(\\int \\sec^2 (3x)\\, \\textrm{d}x -\\int1 \\, \\textrm{d}x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t                                &= 2\\left( \\dfrac{1}{3}\\tan (3x) -x\\right)+C\\\n                                                                 &= \t\\dfrac{2}{3}\\tan (3x) -2x+C.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139534439 true] [17592186096749 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288278 13194139534439 true] [17592186096749 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288281 13194139534439 true] [17592186096749 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288285 13194139534439 true] [17592186096750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288257 13194139534439 true] [175921860